/* Atom language pack (en, site). Generated by scripts/i18n/i18n_tool.py build from i18n/en.json; edit that file, not this one. */
AtomI18N.load("en",{"meta":{"name":"English","dir":"ltr","script":"latin","voice":"en-US","salt":0},"slice":"site","s":{"10cwico":"Heat {name}, or run a current through it, and it glows in exactly these colors. They are the same everywhere in the universe. That is how we know what the stars are made of. Each line is one electron dropping between two levels. The photon carries away the difference. {LNOTE}","10vrohx":"What you have made your own. A ring marks an element you have explored. A half-filled dot marks one you have built. A solid dot marks one you have mastered in the labs.","13d03ab":"Fall into <span data-el=\"lname\">iron</span> one scroll at a time. See its electron cloud, the light it gives off, the empty space inside it, its nucleus, a proton, and the quarks you can try to pull out. Every picture is computed live.","14ci857":"The dark gaps are <b>nodes</b>. There the wave passes through zero, and the electron is never found. A {orb} orbital has {nr, plural, =0 {no spherical nodes} one {# spherical node} other {# spherical nodes}} and {l} through the middle. That is n − 1 = {all} in all. In a full subshell the flat ones cancel out. That is why it is round.","153n5px":"The Big Bang should have made equal amounts of matter and antimatter. Antihydrogen falls down, not up (measured in 2023, to about 20%). The missing asymmetry is still missing.","17f68t":"What each element is helping build right now. Pick a field to light up its elements. Every card ends with an open problem that someone could take on.","17jkn1b":"Rough shares of the Solar System's supply, after Johnson (2019). Merging neutron stars stand in for all rapid neutron capture. Rare supernovae may make some of it.","17vmrf5":"Keep falling, into {name}. Its atom is about {fmt} picometers across. Its nucleus is {fmt2} femtometers. That is {fmt3} times smaller. Make the nucleus a marble, and the atom would be {fmt4} meters wide. That is about {fields} football {fields, plural, one {field} other {fields}} end to end.","1955ya5":"Radiocarbon dating reaches back about 50,000 years. After that, too little carbon-14 is left to count. For older rocks, geologists use slower clocks, such as uranium-238. Its half-life is 4.5 billion years.","1ao5m9t":"The page is lit by {el}’s own light. The rings around the atom are its lines, spread out by wavelength, the way a diffraction grating spreads them. Violet is nearest, red is farthest.","1c0wxbn":"That is {est} MeV per nucleon from the liquid-drop formula. The measured value is {meas} MeV. Building it released that energy. So the nucleus weighs {pct}% less than its loose parts: E = mc².","1d0zkzg":"That is {est} MeV per nucleon from the liquid-drop formula. Building it released that energy. So the nucleus weighs {pct}% less than its loose parts: E = mc².","1ddf9hf":"Keep falling, into {name}. Nobody has made enough of it to measure how big its atom is. But its nucleus is about {fmt} femtometers across. The cloud around it is tens of thousands of times wider.","1hcwy4z":"The dark gaps are <b>nodes</b>. There the wave passes through zero, and the electron is never found. A {orb} orbital has {nr, plural, =0 {no spherical nodes} one {# spherical node} other {# spherical nodes}} and {l} through the middle. That is n − 1 = {all} in all.","1kfznqa":"In a bottle, neutrons last 877.8 seconds. In a beam, 887.7 seconds. That is ten seconds apart, about four standard deviations. A 2024 beam experiment at J-PARC counted electrons instead of protons. It agrees with the bottles. So the suspect is the proton-counting beams.","1lso4zq":"It snapped at {d} fm. The string held {Q} GeV. That is enough to make a {pair} quark and its antiquark out of the vacuum. The antiquark left with the quark you pulled ({meson}, {mesonQuarks}). The new quark took its place ({after}, {baryonQuarks}). Charge in, charge out: the same.","1ozcqzs":"Drop hydrogen&rsquo;s electron from level 3 to level 2. The energy leaves as one photon of red light. For a one-electron atom that is the Rydberg formula. Every line below is computed from it:","1pg18j9":"Learn it by doing it. Build atoms and molecules. Explore the table's patterns. Work through the math of any atom. Test yourself. Run real experiments. Your progress stays in this browser.","1rs7f6z":"Binding energy per nucleon, in MeV, against the number of nucleons. The dots are measured masses. The line is the liquid-drop formula above. The white dot is <span data-el=\"lname\">iron</span>.","1s4814b":"Splitting one uranium-235 nucleus releases about 200 million electronvolts. That is millions of times more than burning one molecule of fuel. Reactors make nearly a tenth of the world's electricity this way.","1uwshd2":"Add two neutrons to make carbon-14. Cosmic rays make it in the air. It decays at a steady rate, and that is the clock that dates wood and bone.","1xierq9":"<b>New in 2026:</b> clocks that tick on a change inside a nucleus. A laser first excited thorium-229 in 2024. In October 2026, two groups reported working nuclear clocks.","1yb22i5":"Fall into silicon's electron cloud. It has four outer electrons. Add a trace of an element with three or five, and it becomes a conductor that a voltage can switch on and off.","1ynjgpl":"Pick technetium. It has no stable form at all. Yet technetium-99m is used in about four out of five nuclear medicine scans. That is tens of millions a year.","333t0z":"Everything you have ever touched is some number of these three particles. Protons decide the element. Neutrons decide whether it lasts. Electrons decide how it bonds.","7b5edk":"Open uranium's card and find it on the binding-energy curve. Past iron, each nucleon is held a little less tightly. So breaking a heavy nucleus in two releases energy.","7zl9de":"Where each element was made. Most of the hydrogen and helium are from the Big Bang. Everything heavier was made in stars, in their explosions, and in their collisions. Pick a source to see its share.","a5u79w":"The app opens on the same table and goes deeper. It has every orbital of every element, the nuclear shell model, decay chains, and a guided tour. It is a single file of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. It has no network access and no tracking. Run it on a Mac, on Windows, or on Linux, or open it in any modern browser. It is free and open source.","af1ya9":"These are measured lines from the NIST Atomic Spectra Database, converted from air to vacuum wavelengths. With more than one electron, the electrons push on each other. Then the Schrödinger equation has no exact solution. So these are measured, not computed.","co5ubm":"The electron that puts it in the {blk} block sits in a <b>{orbName}</b> orbital. That subshell is full, so it is perfectly round. The inner electrons shield it from the nucleus. So it feels a smaller charge (Slater&rsquo;s rules, an estimate):","ebzo4w":"Every element glows in its own set of colors. Atom does not carry {el}&rsquo;s measured lines yet. So here is hydrogen, the one atom whose spectrum is exact arithmetic.","g6zh3n":"Keep pulling. Near 1.25 fm the string holds about 1.1 GeV. That is more than it costs to make a quark and an antiquark out of the vacuum. So it snaps instead of stretching.","hv36wf":"This is how the Sun begins. The weak force has to turn a proton into a neutron in the middle of a collision. So a proton in the Sun's core waits billions of years, on average.","je3xlw":"Turn the temperature up to at least 10 keV, about 116 million kelvin, and fire again. Watch where the Gamow peak sits. Most reactions happen far below the barrier, by tunneling.","liiezq":"Read the chance to tunnel. On top of that, a proton must turn into a neutron by the weak force as they touch. So an average proton in the Sun's core waits billions of years. Fire once more.","lrrn5d":"This is hydrogen's one electron in its ground state, 1s. It is computed, not drawn: <b>ψ = R(r) · Y(θ, φ)</b>, a radial wave times a spherical one. The brightness is how likely you are to find the electron there. Hydrogen is the only atom whose equation can be solved exactly.","oyxvq9":"Per nucleon, that binding peaks near iron and nickel. Fusing light nuclei climbs the curve from one side. Splitting heavy ones climbs it from the other. That is the whole story of starlight and of reactors.","pqgzr9":"<b>σ = 0.90 GeV/fm</b> is the string tension. One GeV per femtometer is 1.602 × 10⁵ newtons, about 16.3 metric tons of force. So this string pulls with <b>14.7 metric tons</b>, no matter how long it is. <b>L<sub>min</sub></b> is the total length of the three legs. They meet at a point where every angle is 120°. A = 0.137 is the short-range part, which works like an electric pull.","q8depu":"Elements 119 and 120 would start a new row of the table. Nobody has made either one yet. Whether the heaviest nuclei have an island of stability is still an open question.","soks1b":"The dark gaps in an orbital are <b>nodes</b>. There the wave passes through zero, and the electron is never found. The ground state has none. Here hydrogen is excited to 3p: one spherical node and one flat one. That is n − 1 = 2 in all.","vq4j47":"No quark ever comes out alone. The energy you put in turns into new matter instead. That is confinement. Proving that the theory behind it has a mass gap is a million-dollar problem.","zaekrm":"The electron that puts it in the {blk} block sits in a <b>{orbName}</b> orbital. The inner electrons shield it from the nucleus. So it feels a smaller charge (Slater&rsquo;s rules, an estimate):","zaooji":"The green line at 558 nm is the aurora. It is a 'forbidden' transition. It is so slow that only the thin upper air lets it shine."}});
